Two parts: the data that has to cross from the duel into the run state, and the row that renders it.

PART A, plumbing. The honest minimum is two numbers; finishDuel (duel-actions.ts:79-95) needs no changes since duel.tsx's finish() already has access to the same state finishDuel read.

1. duel.tsx, the DuelFinish interface (currently lines 67-72): add two fields.
```
export interface DuelFinish {
  won: boolean;
  chip: number;
  capWin: boolean;
  cellsUsed: number;
  overRotations: number;
  trapsFired: number;
}
```
2. duel.tsx, the finish function (currently lines 406-415): populate them straight from state, mirroring finishDuel's own formula (duel-actions.ts:86-90) so the UI redisplays strain math rather than re-deriving it.
```
const finish = () => {
  if (finishedRef.current) return;
  finishedRef.current = true;
  onFinish({
    won: state.phase === "won",
    chip: state.strainChip,
    capWin: state.winKind === "cap",
    cellsUsed: kit.patchCells - state.patchCells,
    overRotations: Math.max(0, state.econ.player.rotations - state.par),
    trapsFired: state.econ.player.trapsFired,
  });
};
```
3. shop-os.tsx, the dive's onFinish callback (currently lines 327-330): pass the two new fields through to the duelFinished dispatch alongside the existing four.
4. run-reducer.ts: add `overRotations: number; trapsFired: number;` to the duelFinished variant of RunAction (currently line 32), and to the lastResult object built in the duelFinished case (currently lines 253-261), copying action.overRotations and action.trapsFired straight across, same pattern as the existing chip/capWin fields.
5. save.ts: add the same two fields to the lastResult shape on RunState (currently lines 91-100).

PART B, the row. screens.tsx, ResultScreen's NEURAL STRAIN row (currently lines 376-381). Leave the summary line exactly as is (still the true total, still reads clean at zero); add an itemized list under it, rendered only when r.chip > 0, one li per nonzero contributor, using finishDuel's exact per-unit multipliers:
```
<div>
  <span>NEURAL STRAIN</span>
  <em className={r.chip > 0 ? "kp-chip-bad" : ""}>
    {r.chip > 0 ? `-${r.chip}` : "clean"} ({run.strain} left)
  </em>
</div>
{r.chip > 0 && (
  <ul className="kp-chip-breakdown">
    {r.overRotations > 0 && (
      <li>
        <span>{r.overRotations} rotation{r.overRotations === 1 ? "" : "s"} over par</span>
        <em>-{r.overRotations * 2}</em>
      </li>
    )}
    {r.trapsFired > 0 && (
      <li>
        <span>{r.trapsFired} trap{r.trapsFired === 1 ? "" : "s"} sprung</span>
        <em>-{r.trapsFired * 4}</em>
      </li>
    )}
    {r.capWin && (
      <li>
        <span>hit the turn cap</span>
        <em>-10</em>
      </li>
    )}
    {r.overRotations * 2 + r.trapsFired * 4 + (r.capWin ? 10 : 0) > 40 && (
      <li className="kp-chip-capped">
        <span>strain billed, capped</span>
        <em>-40 max</em>
      </li>
    )}
  </ul>
)}
```
CSS, add near the existing .kp-result-rows / .kp-chip-bad block (currently around lines 1054-1100):
```
.kp-chip-breakdown {
  list-style: none;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 4px;
  margin-top: 6px;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  background: var(--kp-bg1);
  border: 1px dashed var(--kp-line);
  max-width: 460px;
}
.kp-chip-breakdown li {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 12px;
  font-family: var(--kp-term);
  font-size: 14px;
  color: var(--kp-dim);
}
.kp-chip-breakdown li em {
  font-style: normal;
  color: var(--kp-crimson);
}
.kp-chip-capped, .kp-chip-capped em {
  color: var(--kp-gold);
}
```
No new sound: the chip already gets no dedicated sfx today (the win/lose stinger via playStinger covers the moment, audio.ts), and a result row reading itself in more detail is not a new event.

One mechanism, a long-press detector plus a matching inline popover, covers every plain-title tip and the mid-dive ability-info panel. New file components/game/tap-tip.tsx (sibling to components/game/teach.tsx, the game's other cross-cutting overlay component), exporting two things.

useLongPress({ isOpen, onOpen, onClose }), returning pointer and click handlers to spread onto a wrapping element:
- Every handler no-ops immediately unless e.pointerType === "touch", so mouse and pen are byte-for-byte unaffected.
- onPointerDown: records the start position, starts a 400ms timer (LONG_PRESS_MS).
- onPointerMove: cancels the timer if the pointer has moved more than 10px (LONG_PRESS_SLOP_PX) since down, treating that as a scroll or drag rather than a press.
- Timer fires: calls onOpen() and marks the following click for suppression.
- onPointerUp: if the timer never fired (a genuine short tap) and isOpen was already true, calls onClose() and marks the following click for suppression too (this is the second-tap-dismisses behavior); otherwise this is a normal tap and the native click proceeds untouched, so casting a program or selecting a mode still works exactly as today.
- onPointerCancel / onPointerLeave: clears a pending timer without opening anything.
- onContextMenu: always calls preventDefault(), so a long press never pops the mobile text-selection or context-menu callout.
- onClickCapture: if the preceding press was marked for suppression, calls preventDefault() and stopPropagation(), then clears the mark. stopPropagation matters even when the tip-bearing element is an inert span nested inside a clickable ancestor (e.g. the THREAT pips inside the job card's own button, screens.tsx:110-115): stopping it during the capture phase, before the event reaches the target, keeps a long press on the pip from also firing pickJob on the card underneath it.

TapTip({ text, children }), a thin wrapper: local open state wired to useLongPress, rendering
```
<span className="kp-tapwrap" title={text} {...handlers}>
  {children}
  {open && <span className="kp-tap-pop" role="tooltip">{text}</span>}
</span>
```
and, only while open, one document pointerdown capture listener that closes the tip when the tap lands outside the wrapper (tap-outside-dismisses). Renders children bare, no wrapper, when text is undefined.

Wiring, the seven plain-title sites named in the finding, each the same edit: drop the inline title=... prop (the wrapper now owns it) and wrap the element in <TapTip text={...same expression...}>:
- duel.tsx:458, the PAR span, text={tip("par")}
- duel.tsx:463, the .kp-strain div, text={tip("strain")}
- screens.tsx:126, the .kp-job-tier span, text={tip("threatTier")}
- screens.tsx:137, the STRAIN span in JobBoard's footer, text={tip("strain")}
- screens.tsx:139, the RAM span in the same footer, text={tip("ram")}
- screens.tsx:271, the ATTACK mode button, text={owned ? attackModeDesc(m, kit.attackTier) : tip("modeLocked")}
- screens.tsx:298, the DEFEND mode button, text={owned ? defendModeDesc(m, kit.defendTier) : tip("modeLocked")}

Wiring, the ability-info panel (duel.tsx, the three .kp-ability buttons at roughly lines 588-609, and the shared .kp-ability-info panel at 652-657): reuse useLongPress directly against the existing lifted infoProg state instead of a local open, so the same panel that already serves mouse hover also serves a touch hold. Add data-prog={prog} to each button (needed for the outside-dismiss check below). Per button:
```
const lp = useLongPress({
  isOpen: infoProg === prog,
  onOpen: () => setInfoProg(prog),
  onClose: () => setInfoProg(null),
});
```
spread {...lp} onto the button alongside its existing onMouseEnter / onMouseLeave / onFocus / onBlur / onClick, all unchanged. Also add one line to the top of onProgram (duel.tsx, currently lines 377-404): `if (infoProg) setInfoProg(null);`, so casting any program also clears a lingering info panel regardless of how it was opened. Add one effect scoped so it can never fire from a mouse-driven open: a ref set true only inside a button's onOpen and cleared in onClose; while true and infoProg is non-null, a document pointerdown capture listener closes infoProg whenever the tap lands outside the currently-open button (matched via its data-prog attribute). This gives the panel the same tap-elsewhere-dismisses behavior the inline tips get, without adding a second dismiss path that could ever fight the mouse one.

CSS, add near the other floating-panel styles (e.g. after .kp-ability-info, currently around line 2276):
```
.kp-tapwrap {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  -webkit-touch-callout: none;
  -webkit-user-select: none;
  user-select: none;
  touch-action: manipulation;
}
.kp-tap-pop {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  left: 0;
  margin-top: 6px;
  z-index: 45;
  max-width: 220px;
  padding: 8px 10px;
  background: rgba(10, 12, 16, 0.97);
  border: 2px solid var(--kp-signal);
  color: var(--kp-text);
  font-family: var(--kp-term);
  font-size: 15px;
  line-height: 1.3;
  white-space: normal;
  pointer-events: none;
  animation: kp-teach-in 140ms steps(3, end);
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .kp-tap-pop { animation: none; }
}
```
(kp-teach-in already exists, styles.css:1863, an opacity-only fade; reused rather than adding a new keyframe.) Also add `-webkit-touch-callout: none; -webkit-user-select: none; touch-action: manipulation;` to the existing .kp-ability rule (currently around line 1488), since it is now long-press-sensitive too and needs the same callout suppression as .kp-tapwrap.

This is the smallest version that closes the finding: one new file, the seven named sites, plus the ability dock, no layout change, no new dependency, zero visible change for a mouse. What it deliberately leaves for a fuller version later, because doing it now would mean either a redesign or a new dependency: (1) no discoverability affordance, a touch player has no visual hint any of these controls hold more information, the gesture has to be found rather than seen, and a dotted underline or dot on tip-bearing controls would close this but is itself a chrome change to controls this brief asked to leave alone; (2) no viewport-edge awareness, kp-tap-pop always opens below-left of its wrapper and can clip near the right or bottom edge (e.g. the rightmost dock ability), a fuller version would flip the anchor on overflow; (3) no cross-control exclusivity, two tips could theoretically both be open under multitouch, rare enough not to warrant a shared singleton this pass; (4) no haptic confirmation (navigator.vibrate) on trigger, a common mobile affordance, cut to keep this self-contained and dependency-free.
